ew York City: results from a post-World Trade Center disaster survey</article-title><source>J Urban Health</source><volume>82</volume><fpage>76</fpage><lpage>89</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15738333</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b48-ehp-116-1383"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wheeler</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>McKelvey</surname><given-names>W</given-names></name><name><surname>Thorpe</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Perrin</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Cone</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Kass</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2007</year><article-title>Asthma diagnosed after September 11, 2001 among rescue and recovery workers: findings from the World Trade Center Health Registry</article-title><source>Environ Health Perspect</source><volume>115</volume><fpage>1584</fpage><lpage>1590</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18007989</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b49-ehp-116-1383"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wong</surname><given-names>EY</given-names></name><name><surname>Gohlke</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Griffith</surname><given-names>WC</given-names></name><name><surname>Farrow</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Faustima</surname><given-names>EM</given-names></name></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Assessing the health benefits of air pollution reduction for children</article-title><source>Environ Health Perspect</source><volume>112</volume><fpage>226</fpage><lpage>232</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14754578</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b50-ehp-116-1383"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wright</surname><given-names>RJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Rodriguez</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Cohen</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><year>1998</year><article-title>Review of psychosocial stress and asthma: an integrated biopsychosocial approach</article-title><source>Thorax</source><volume>53</volume><fpage>1066</fpage><lpage>1074</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10195081</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="f1-ehp-116-1383" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Map showing lower Manhattan in New York City: site of WTC, Canal Street (northern boundary of recruitment for WTCHR), and Chambers Street.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ehp-116-1383f1"/></fig><fig id="f2-ehp-116-1383" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Exposures related to the WTC 9/11 disaster, reported for 3,184 children enrolled in WTCHR: percent exposed by enrollment group. &#x0201c;Neither resident nor student&#x0201d; indicates 74 youths who worked in rescue/recovery and 80 passers-by south of Chambers Street on the morning of 11 September 2001. &#x0201c;Witnessed any disturbing event&#x0201d; included personally seeing an airplane hitting the WTC, people falling or jumping from the WTC, buildings collapsing, people running away from a cloud or smoke, or people injured or killed.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ehp-116-1383f2"/></fig><table-wrap id="t1-ehp-116-1383" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Demographic and exposure characteristics of 3,184 children enrolled in the WTCHR, September 2003 through November 2004 [no. (%)].</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Category</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Resident</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Nonresident student</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Neither<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="tfn1-ehp-116-1383">a</xref></th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Total</th><